From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emmanuel Velez Garcia <velezgarciaemmanuel25@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 08:54:34 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Attribute blocking chunk loads, and size the chunk I/O pool

Async chunk I/O in 1.8 covers exactly one caller: the player chunk map,
and only for chunks that already exist on disk. Everything else - a mine
reset, a schematic paste, a hopper reaching across a border, any plugin
touching a block - goes through originalGetChunkAt and reads, or worse
generates, inside the tick. The counters added with the profiler say how
often that happens; they cannot say who did it, and who did it is the
part you can act on.

/vs perf trace on captures a stack on each blocking load and charges it
to the first frame outside the server, keeping the deepest server frame
alongside so the path is readable:

     412 ms    38 loads, 12 generated  MineResetTask.run:88 <- World.getType

Tracing is off by default and toggleable at runtime, because it is real
work on a hot path - about ten microseconds against a disk read costing
hundreds. Cheap next to what it measures, not free.

The chunk I/O pool is now configurable (chunkIo.minThreads,
playersPerThread, maxThreads) rather than fixed at two threads plus one
per fifty players. Its player-count division was also two ints inside
Math.ceil, so the ceil never rounded anything up and 199 players got
three threads instead of four; that is now a floating point division.

More I/O threads help on an SSD and do nothing on a spinning disk, so
the comment says to raise it only against a measurement.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

